> - `verifier.py` — `Verifier.verify(result, previous) -> Verification` measuring **accuracy**, **regression** against the previous round and **consistency** over the 3 runs; passes only at accuracy ≥ 90%, 0 regressions, all consistent; accuracy is the `verification_score` (**FR-CAP-009**, ADR-010). Per **FR-CAP-010** this proves correctness on the test cases only, **not** formal verification. The LLM is never fine-tuned — only the generated tool is refined
> - `registry.py` — `Registry.register(name, version, code, score) -> Capability` (**FR-CAP-013**), also saving the research notes and test cases in the `research_notes` / `test_cases` columns from Prompt 10, with the `BUILDING / VERIFIED / FAILED` lifecycle from Architecture §15.6. Only `VERIFIED` rows are ever handed to an agent; `FAILED` rows are **kept**, never deleted (**FR-CAP-014**, BR-10)
> - `engine.py` — `CapabilityEngine.resolve_gap(capability_name, context) -> Capability | None`, running the stages in the order shown in Architecture §13.1 (**FR-CAP-001**, ADR-008): **registry check → research → build → static import check → sandbox → test → verify → register**. A `VERIFIED` registry match skips straight to resume (**FR-CAP-016**). A free tool named by the research notes skips only the build — it still goes through static check, sandbox, test and verify (**FR-CAP-004**)
